                <h2>Conclusion First: Compliance Audits Are Insurance, Not Cost</h2>
<p>Many teams treat compliance audits as "optional process optimization" until one morning they open their ad dashboard to find the account banned and balance cleared. My assessment is: <strong>the ROI of compliance audits is extremely high—one audit can prevent losses that might equal a week's labor costs.</strong></p>
<p>This conclusion comes from real operational data. A utility app team had their Meta account banned twice within three months due to creative violations, resulting in 12 days of paused campaigns. Based on their ROI at the time, direct losses exceeded $150,000, not counting competitor gains during recovery. Another cross-border e-commerce team received regulatory letters due to missing privacy policies on landing pages, with a rectification period lasting a full month. The common thread: both teams had compliance awareness but lacked systematic audit mechanisms.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Overseas Ad Compliance Audit SOP (Ready to Implement)</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Creative Compliance Check (Mandatory Before Every Launch)</h3>
<p>Creatives are high-risk violation areas and the audit priority. Checklist:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Copy self-check</strong>: Any absolute statements ("best," "only," "guaranteed")? Any implications of therapeutic or preventive claims? Any disparaging content toward competitors?</li>
<li><strong>Image/video compliance</strong>: Any unauthorized brand logos or copyrighted materials? Portrait rights obtained? No violence, pornography, or discriminatory elements?</li>
<li><strong>Authenticity of social proof</strong>: User review screenshots from authorized real users? Data claims with third-party backing?</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Operational experience</strong>: Approval rates aren't mysterious. A feed ad team found that after implementing the creative compliance checklist, per-creative approval rates increased from 67% to 89%, reducing appeals by 3-4 operations daily on average.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h3>Step 2: Audience Targeting Compliance Review</h3>
<p>This step is most often overlooked during rapid iteration. High-frequency violation points:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Age targeting</strong>: Are age restrictions (18+ or 21+) set for education, finance, or healthcare products?</li>
<li><strong>Sensitive attributes</strong>: Ad platforms explicitly prohibit targeting or exclusions based on race, religion, gender, sexual orientation, or health status. If audience profiles touch these dimensions, targeting logic needs re-evaluation.</li>
<li><strong>Retargeting lists</strong>: Are users with negative feedback excluded?</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Practical advice</strong>: When creating audiences in Meta Ads Manager or Google Ads, add a verification node at the "Save Audience" stage—have someone other than the creator confirm no sensitive attributes are involved.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 3: Landing Page Compliance Verification</h3>
<p>Creatives pass review, but landing pages fall short—this is more common than you'd think. Key areas:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Privacy policy</strong>: Does it clearly state data collection scope, usage purpose, and user rights? Compliant with GDPR (EU), CCPA (California), etc.?</li>
<li><strong>Unsubscribe/cancellation</strong>: Do subscription products offer clear cancellation paths? Clear notice before free trial converts to paid?</li>
<li><strong>Contact information</strong>: Does the landing page include real brand contact (email or address)?</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Common oversight</strong>: Many teams only focus on conversion rates after landing pages go live, overlooking the legal notice area in the footer. One dropshipper nearly had their account banned after users reported the "Terms of Service" link led to a 404 page, flagged as "deceptive advertising."</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 4: Data Tracking Compliance Confirmation</h3>
<p>Overseas ad platforms have strict restrictions on user data usage. Verify:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pixel/SDK configuration</strong>: Used only for ad attribution, not building cross-site user profiles?</li>
<li><strong>Event tracking</strong>: Do sensitive events (e.g., "credit card registered," "purchase completed") fire only after explicit user consent?</li>
<li><strong>Third-party tools</strong>: Are data-sharing agreements for Google Analytics, Mixpanel, etc. compliant?</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>A Tool Recommendation: Audit Log Sheet</h2>
<p>Maintain a shared "Compliance Audit Log" documenting every creative launch or account structure change:</p>
